judge_raw_output:
The analyzer shows the optimizer is improving but still produces fragile designs: robust_performance_mc mean 0.3949 (std 0.0906, best 0.4720) while nominal_performance mean 0.5049 (std 0.1691) and some candidates have nominal=0. Safety is marginal: cliff_margin mean 0.0772 (std 0.0721) with a negative minimum -0.0947. The simplest, evidence-backed fix is to change the objectives: increase safety pressure, de-emphasize nominal-only gains, and add a center-distance objective to favor centrally located, noise-resilient designs.

<decision>{
  "failure_location": "objective",
  "root_cause_hypothesis": "Current objective weighting under-emphasizes safety and centrality: nominal_performance (weight=1.0) still incentivizes high nominal but fragile designs near the cliff, while cliff_margin (weight=0.8) is too weak to prevent some candidates crossing the failure boundary (cliff_margin min = -0.0947). No explicit centrality objective encourages off-center designs that amplify noise sensitivity.",
  "intervention_target": "objective",
  "rationale": "Analyzer data: robust_performance_mc mean=0.3949 (std=0.0906, best=0.4720) while nominal_performance mean=0.5049 (std=0.1691) and cliff_margin mean=0.0772 (std=0.0721) with worst=-0.0947. Raising cliff_margin weight and lowering nominal_performance weight will prioritize safety over fragile nominal gains. Adding center_distance (minimize) with modest weight should bias designs toward the center (0.5,..) and reduce sensitivity to symmetric Gaussian manufacturing noise (std=0.08).",
  "proposed_change": {
    "ops": [
      {"op": "set_weight", "name": "nominal_performance", "weight": 0.5},
      {"op": "set_weight", "name": "cliff_margin", "weight": 1.2},
      {"op": "add_from_registry", "name": "center_distance", "optimization_direction": "minimize", "weight": 0.5}
    ]
  },
  "expected_benefit": "Fewer candidates near or beyond the failure boundary (reduce negative cliff_margin instances), improved robustness of top candidates (higher robust_performance_mc mean and/or best), and reduced variance in safety margin by encouraging central, less noise-sensitive designs. This should accelerate finding designs with higher expected performance under the given Gaussian tolerances.",
  "validation_plan": "The validator should compare the next accepted-state performance statistics to the current state (robust_performance_mc mean=0.3949, best=0.4720; cliff_margin mean=0.0772, min=-0.0947). Success if robust_performance_mc (mean or best) improves or if unsafe candidates (negative cliff_margin) decrease while not reducing robust_performance_mc.",
  "confidence": 0.7
}</decision>

The population shows occasional nominal failures (nominal_performance worst = 0.0; cliff_margin worst = -0.0079) and modest average safety margin (cliff_margin mean = 0.1065). The analyzer recommended stronger safety emphasis and a slight relaxation of the center-distance pressure to allow safe exploration. I will make a minimal, targeted objective-weight change to push the optimizer toward safer nominal designs while preserving robust_performance_mc as primary.

<decision>{
  "failure_location": "objective",
  "root_cause_hypothesis": "Safety is under-weighted relative to the robust_performance objective, causing occasional nominal failures despite clustering near center; MC noise worsens selection but the immediate fix is stronger safety weighting.",
  "intervention_target": "objective",
  "rationale": "Analyzer reports at least one nominal failure (nominal_performance = 0.0) and cliff_margin worst = -0.0079 while cliff_margin mean is only 0.1065. Robust_performance_mc is already dominant (weight 3.0) and noisy (SD 0.0524). Increasing cliff_margin weight should reduce nominal failures; reducing center_distance weight slightly will allow the optimizer to explore nearby safe regions that may improve robust performance (analyzer suggested ~2.0 for safety and lowering center_distance from 0.5 to ~0.2).",
  "proposed_change": {
    "ops": [
      {
        "op": "set_weight",
